# Every flow node that belongs in a lane, with the layer whose lane it is. One row per
# (filing, layer, node), over both kinds of node a translation emits.
#
# A node count misses an orphan
#   An emission that put every call activity outside every lane once passed
#   `|callActivity| = |part|` exactly. A BPMN reader of such a diagram sees nodes floating in a
#   process and cannot tell which layer any of them feeds, so F's incidence is gone from the
#   rendering while the cardinality law is perfectly satisfied. That is a diagram returning the
#   right shape and a believable count, which is the whole failure mode `diagrams/roster.sqlc`
#   exists to catch.
#
# Two kinds of node, labelled by the caller
#   They land in a lane for two different reasons:
#     a draw   an operation sits in the lane of the layer it draws from. D's incidence.
#     a part   a call sits in the lane of the layer it is composed into. F's incidence.
#   Neither arm knows which law it serves; the caller is the thing holding both.
#
# An induce-only operation has no lane
#   An operation that only induces has no lane here, and that is not an omission. A draw lane set
#   can only place what draws, so a notation cannot site an operation that merely commits something
#   on another layer. Every operation in this corpus draws, so the case is unexercised; the relation
#   binds it anyway, because a future filing may not.
-- entries/draws.sqlc and diagrams/foreign_calls.sqlc, each projected onto the lane it belongs to.
SELECT 'draw' AS node_kind, d.filing, d.layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/lane_members.sqlc)
) d
UNION ALL
SELECT 'part', c.composition, c.composed_layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/foreign_calls.sqlc)
) c
